---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { asOf } from "../lib/data.ts";
import { breadcrumbLd, faqLd } from "../lib/seo.ts";
import { LABELS, SITE } from "../lib/site.ts";

const faqs = [
  {
    q: "How are the two stories chosen?",
    a: "Every morning an automated scan searches accepted US news publishers and the odd-news wires for recent stories. It proposes up to four candidates for each side, ranked; on the facepalm side the most absurd is tried first. Each candidate is then checked independently; the first on each side to pass every check is published. A story qualifies if the event happened, was announced or was published within the previous seven days for genius, or fourteen days for facepalm, whatever the date on the news pages. Odd news is often picked up by the wires a week or more after a local outlet breaks it. If either side has no survivor, nothing is published that day.",
  },
  {
    q: "What counts as a genius story?",
    a: "A specific, completed, verifiable achievement in the United States: a scientific result, an engineering or medical first, a major prize, a record, a rescue or civic fix that took real ingenuity. Not product launches, funding rounds, stock moves, marketing or predictions.",
  },
  {
    q: "What counts as a facepalm story?",
    a: "A specific, verifiable act of human absurdity in the United States that was its own consequence, reported straight by credible outlets: first a person's own baffling act, then an official decision that is absurd on its face. Routine administrative errors such as ballot misprints no longer run. Never a story where anyone was killed, injured or hospitalised, where the subject is under 18 or a victim of a crime, where the behaviour suggests a mental-health crisis, addiction or disability, or where the joke is poverty, immigration status, religion or a group of people.",
  },
  {
    q: "Who fact-checks the stories?",
    a: "Three layers. Deterministic code rejects anything whose sources break the sourcing rules, that lacks two independent news organisations, that uses loaded wording, or that trips the no-victims rules. A second, adversarial model then tries to reject the story and must approve it; if the facts are sound but our wording is imprecise, it supplies corrected wording, which is re-checked by the same rules. Finally, two verbatim quotes, one from each publisher, are matched mechanically against the live pages. A story that fails any step is not published, and the scan log says why.",
  },
  {
    q: "Are politicians included?",
    a: "Yes, for concrete acts in office that two news organisations reported as news, never for their views. The site keeps the running count of political facepalm stories about Democrats and Republicans within one of each other; when one side is ahead, the scan may not run another story about that party until the other catches up.",
  },
  {
    q: "What is the pair of the week?",
    a: "For each Monday-to-Sunday week, the day whose two stories the fact-checker scored highest for how strongly they show each side. It is chosen by arithmetic from that week's daily pairs, not by an editor, and it can change until the week ends.",
  },
  {
    q: "What if a story is wrong?",
    a: `Open an issue on GitHub. A day can be corrected or removed by hand, and every correction is logged on that day's page. The scan can never edit or delete a published day on its own.`,
  },
];
---

<Base
  title={`Methodology · ${SITE.name}`}
  description="How the daily pair is found, what qualifies for each side, the no-victims rules, and the three layers of fact-checking every story passes before it is published."
  path="/methodology"
  ogImage="/og/methodology.png"
  ogImageAlt="Methodology"
  modified={asOf}
  jsonLd={[breadcrumbLd([{ name: "Methodology", path: "/methodology" }]), faqLd(faqs)]}
>
  <div class="day-head">
    <h1>Methodology</h1>
    <p class="tagline">How stories are chosen and checked</p>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      {SITE.name} publishes one pair of true stories a day. Both happened in the United States. Both
      were reported as news by at least two news organisations. Both are summarised in our own
      words, without commentary, and linked to their sources. The labels are ours; the facts are
      theirs.
    </p>

    <h2>What qualifies</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>{LABELS.genius}.</strong> A specific, completed, verifiable achievement by a person,
      team or institution: a scientific result, an engineering or medical first, a Nobel, MacArthur,
      Lasker, Breakthrough or Pulitzer prize, a record, a rescue or civic fix that took real
      ingenuity, a child or amateur who did something experts could not. Not product launches,
      funding rounds, stock prices, marketing, celebrity news, opinion or "could one day"
      speculation. A press release alone is never enough.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>{LABELS.facepalm}.</strong> A specific, verifiable act of human absurdity that was its
      own consequence, reported straight by credible outlets, that a reader will recognise without
      being told. In order of preference: a person's own voluntary, baffling act; then an official
      or institutional decision that is absurd on its face, such as a council that bans something
      that doesn't exist or a bill that misspells the state. A crime counts when the only person it
      harms is the one who committed it. Routine administrative errors, such as a ballot misprint or
      a payroll mistake, do not count.
    </p>

    <h2>Variety</h2>
    <p>
      For its first month the site ran a county's ballot error almost every day, because those
      stories pass every check easily. They are now limited in code. The fact-checker scores every
      story from 1 to 10 for how strongly it shows its side, and no facepalm story scored below 5 is
      published, which rules out routine administrative errors and mild blunders. No facepalm
      category may run two days in a row. On the facepalm side the candidates are tried in order of
      score, the most absurd first. A day when nothing clears these limits publishes nothing.
    </p>

    <h2>What is never run on the facepalm side</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Any story where someone was killed, injured or hospitalised.</li>
      <li>Any story whose subject is under 18.</li>
      <li>
        Any story whose subject is the victim of a crime, or any crime with a victim other than the
        perpetrator. Drunk or drugged driving and drug cases never run.
      </li>
      <li>
        Any behaviour that suggests a mental-health crisis, addiction, dementia or disability.
      </li>
      <li>
        Any story where the joke is poverty, immigration status, religion or a group of people.
      </li>
      <li>Satire, hoaxes, social-media posts, or anything from a tabloid or aggregator.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Private individuals may be named only when they are adults, the act was their own, and both
      cited sources already name them. Otherwise the act is described without a name.
    </p>

    <h2>Politics</h2>
    <p>
      Politicians and officials are included for what they did or said in office, never for their
      views: a bill, a vote, a ruling, a public statement with consequences, reported by two news
      organisations as news. The site tracks the party of every political facepalm story and keeps
      the counts for Democrats and Republicans within one of each other. When one party is ahead,
      the scan is not allowed to run another story about it until the other catches up.
    </p>

    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <p>
      Every story cites at least two sources from different news organisations, and at least one of
      them is always a news publisher on a fixed accepted list: the wire services, national
      newspapers and broadcasters, major regional papers of record, network-owned local stations,
      public radio and the science press. The second source may be another publisher on that list;
      an official announcement (government, universities, prize bodies); or one local newsroom that
      is not on the list. Odd stories are usually broken by a small-town paper or station and then
      picked up by a wire, and the last option exists so they aren't lost. A local newsroom
      qualifies only if the fact-checker opens the outlet and vouches that it is a real news
      organisation with named reporters doing its own reporting from the place where the story
      happened, not an aggregator, a blog or a rewrite of the wire story. The accepted list is
      public in the repository. Tabloids, satire sites, aggregators, press-release wires, social
      media and Wikipedia are never cited. Each source is also saved to the Wayback Machine when the
      story is published, and the archived copy is linked.
    </p>

    <h2>The three checks</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Rules in code.</strong> The story must fit a strict schema; its sources must follow
        the sourcing rules above and come from two different organisations; its headline and summary
        may not use loaded words; a facepalm story may not mention anyone hurt, anyone under 18, or
        any of the excluded situations. Any failure rejects it before a model is asked.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>An adversarial review.</strong> A second, independent model is given the story and
        told to find reasons it should not run. It opens every source, checks every fact against the
        page, looks into any local newsroom the story relies on, scores the story, and must approve.
        If the facts are sound but our wording is imprecise, it supplies the corrected wording,
        which is then re-checked by the same mechanical rules. "Uncertain" is treated as a
        rejection.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Verbatim quotes.</strong> Each story carries two short quotations, one from each of
        two different publishers. Both are matched mechanically against the live pages. A paraphrase
        fails.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The <a href="/log">scan log</a> lists every candidate the scan considered, and why each one
      was published or set aside. It also names the model that proposed each day's candidates and
      the model that reviewed them. The review is always a separate run from the proposal; where the
      log names two models, it was a different model as well.
    </p>

    <h2 id="pair-of-the-week">Pair of the week</h2>
    <p>
      The <a href="/weeks">pair of the week</a> is the day, in each Monday-to-Sunday week, whose two
      stories have the highest combined score. A tie goes to the day with the stronger facepalm
      story, then to the later day. It is arithmetic over the daily pairs, not a separate selection,
      and it can change until the week is over. Days published before scoring began on November 14,
      2026 are given a neutral score.
    </p>

    <h2>Corrections</h2>
    <p>
      The scan can publish a day; it can never edit or remove one. Corrections are made by hand and
      logged on the day's page. To report an error,{" "}
      <a href={`${SITE.repoUrl}/issues`}>open an issue</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Questions</h2>
    {faqs.map((f) => (
      <>
        <h3>{f.q}</h3>
        <p>{f.a}</p>
      </>
    ))}
  </div>
</Base>
